INIT(8)                        P32W Unix                         INIT(8)

NAME
       init - process control initialization

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/init

DESCRIPTION
       init is process 1, the first program the kernel runs. The
       kernel starts it as root with the arguments { "/etc/init" };
       it is never run by hand. Every program init starts gets the
       environment PATH=/bin:/usr/bin and HOME=/.

       First init opens /dev/tty0, the console, as its standard
       input, output and error. If /etc/rc is readable it runs
       /bin/sh /etc/rc there, as root, and waits for it to finish
       (see rc(8)). /etc/rc need not be executable.

       Then, if /etc/getty is not executable, the system stays
       single-user: init runs a login shell (-sh) on the console as
       root, with no password, and starts a new one each time it
       exits.

       Otherwise init goes multi-user. It closes the console, leaves
       its controlling terminal, and starts /etc/getty tty0 and
       /etc/getty tty1, each in a session of its own, so that each
       terminal becomes the controlling terminal of its login.
       getty(8) asks for a name and becomes login(1), which becomes
       the user's shell: the process init started stays the same
       until the user logs out.

       init then waits for children. When the process it started on
       a terminal exits, init clears that terminal's record in
       /etc/utmp (see utmp(5)) and starts a new getty there. Any
       other child (an orphan whose parent exited before it) is
       simply reaped.

       If a terminal's getty keeps ending at once (5 times in a row
       within 5 seconds of starting, say because /etc/getty cannot
       be executed), init waits a minute before starting the next,
       rather than looping as fast as it can fork.

SIGNALS
       SIGTERM
               stops the respawning: from then on, a terminal whose
               session ends gets no new getty. When the last child
               is gone init pauses for good. shutdown(1) sends this
               before it ends every other process and halts. There
               is no way back to multi-user except a reboot.

       SIGHUP is caught and does nothing (it is caught rather than
       ignored, so that logins do not inherit an ignored SIGHUP).
       Other signals have their default action. If init exits (it
       is killed, or cannot open the console) the kernel panics
       with "init exited".

FILES
       /etc/init      this program
       /etc/rc        the start-up script
       /etc/getty     run on each terminal
       /etc/utmp      who is logged in where
       /dev/tty0, /dev/tty1
                      the terminals

SEE ALSO
       rc(8), getty(8), login(1), shutdown(1), halt(1), utmp(5),
       sh(1)

BUGS
       The terminals are fixed in the program: tty0 and tty1. There
       is no /etc/ttys or /etc/inittab, and no run levels; SIGHUP
       does not make init reread anything.

       The single-user fallback shell asks for no password.
